From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] vrend: zeroed temporaries

The GLSL made from TGSI declares its temporaries (temp<N>, temp<N>[]) without a value. Shaders that come from NIR
(nir_to_tgsi: SPIR-V shaders through GL_ARB_gl_spirv, Mesa's own) can read a temporary no path wrote - an undefined
value, which native drivers usually make zero - and NVIDIA's GLSL compiler leaves it with whatever its register held:
GravityMark's OpenGL frames had saturated blue and black speckles on lit surfaces (infinite or NaN lighting from the
garbage), changing from frame to frame. Temporaries are now declared as zero.
---
--- a/src/vrend/vrend_shader.c	2026-10-26 13:48:39.432380352 +0100
+++ b/src/vrend/vrend_shader.c	2026-10-26 16:12:16.865175049 +0100
@@ -6757,13 +6757,18 @@
    const char *sname = tgsi_proc_to_prefix(ctx->prog_type);
    int glsl_ver_required = ctx->glsl_ver_required;
 
+   /* Zeroed: shaders from NIR (SPIR-V, nir_to_tgsi) read temporaries no path wrote (undefined values), which NVIDIA
+    * leaves with whatever the registers held - GravityMark's OpenGL frames had saturated and black speckles */
    for (i = 0; i < ctx->num_temp_ranges; i++) {
       const char *precise = ctx->temp_ranges[i].precise_result ? "precise" : "";
       if (ctx->temp_ranges[i].array_id > 0) {
-         emit_hdrf(glsl_strbufs, "%s vec4 temp%d[%d];\n", precise, ctx->temp_ranges[i].first,
-                   ctx->temp_ranges[i].last - ctx->temp_ranges[i].first + 1);
+         int len = ctx->temp_ranges[i].last - ctx->temp_ranges[i].first + 1;
+         emit_hdrf(glsl_strbufs, "%s vec4 temp%d[%d] = vec4[%d](", precise, ctx->temp_ranges[i].first, len, len);
+         for (int j = 0; j < len; j++)
+            emit_hdr(glsl_strbufs, j ? ", vec4(0.0)" : "vec4(0.0)");
+         emit_hdr(glsl_strbufs, ");\n");
       } else {
-         emit_hdrf(glsl_strbufs, "%s vec4 temp%d;\n", precise, ctx->temp_ranges[i].first);
+         emit_hdrf(glsl_strbufs, "%s vec4 temp%d = vec4(0.0);\n", precise, ctx->temp_ranges[i].first);
       }
    }
 
